"""Card specs CB01-CB24: car materials, studio lighting, rendering and the pixel stage.
Plan and the reasoning behind every number: docs/art/CB.md. Run by the planner with tools/cards/newcard.py."""

BL = "Blender 5.2 is at `/opt/homebrew/bin/blender`; run scripts as `/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python <script> -- <args>` (arguments after the lone `--`). Blender's Python has bpy, bmesh, mathutils and numpy, NOT PIL. An uncaught exception or `sys.exit(1)` makes the command fail, so tests use plain `assert`. You cannot see images: check files with `file`, `ls -l` and the numbers the tests print."
API = "Blender 5.2 facts already checked: `ShaderNodeMix` with `data_type='RGBA'` has inputs[0]=Factor, inputs[6]=A, inputs[7]=B and outputs[2]=Result; `Light.size_y` works for `shape='RECTANGLE'`; `obj.visible_camera` (bool) hides an object from the camera but it still casts shadows; `scene.render.dither_intensity = 0.0` stops Blender adding noise to 8-bit PNGs (needed for exact mask values); a Boolean modifier solver must be 'EXACT' or 'MANIFOLD' (not 'FAST')."
CARLIB = "tools/art/carlib.py (after V06 `hex_to_rgb('#rrggbb')` returns LINEAR rgb; `make_mat`, `set_socket`, `look_at`, `extrude_polygon`, `polygons`, `load_dossier`, `U = 0.1` metres per car unit)"
PLAN = "docs/art/CB.md (the plan: why every number below was chosen)"
RAMP = "Pixel-stage constants (all cards CB11-CB20 use exactly these): paint ramp multipliers MULT=[0.10,0.20,0.38,0.65,1.0,1.5,2.3]; shadow tint weights SW=[0.35,0.28,0.18,0.08,0,0,0] toward '#2a1a5c'; highlight weights HW=[0,0,0,0,0,0.20,0.55] toward cream '#fff2cc' (toward white '#ffffff' instead when the base colour's linear luma >= 0.5); outline colour '#120a1e'."

CARDS = [
 dict(id="CB01", title="Car materials: paint, chrome, glass, rubber, lamps", needs=["V06"],
  files=["tools/art/cbmat.py", "tests/cb_mat_test.py"],
  goal="One module that builds every car material with fixed numbers (period paint, real chrome, dark glass, rubber, lamp glow), and the table of flat colours used by the material-mask render later.",
  read=[BL, API, CARLIB, "tools/art/carlib.py `make_mat` (copy how it sets sockets), and " + PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/cbmat.py` (imports `carlib` from its own folder like blender_car2.py does; nothing runs on import). Add `MASK_RGB = {'paint':(255,0,0),'chrome':(0,255,0),'glass':(0,0,255),'tyre':(255,255,0),'wall':(255,255,255),'lamp':(255,0,255),'trim':(0,255,255),'tail':(0,0,0),'paint2':(128,0,0)}` (8-bit FILE values, used by card CB05).",
   "Add `make_paint(hexs, finish='solid', name='paint')`: a Principled BSDF with Roughness 0.32, Coat Weight 1.0, Coat Roughness 0.02, Metallic 0.25 when `finish == 'metallic'` else 0.0. Colour flop: a Layer Weight node (Blend 0.5) `Facing` output drives the Factor of a Mix node (data_type 'RGBA'); A = `carlib.hex_to_rgb(hexs)`; B = the same colour with 0.10 added to every channel (capped at 1.0) for 'metallic', or equal to A for 'solid'; the Mix Result goes to Base Color. Name the material `name`.",
   "Add these, each a `carlib.make_mat` call, material name equal to the class name: `make_chrome()` colour '#f2f2f4' metallic 1.0 roughness 0.04; `make_glass()` colour '#1b2830' roughness 0.02 coat 1.0 coat_rough 0.0 (OPAQUE dark glass, alpha 1: it keeps the pixel art crisp and hides the unmodelled interior); `make_tyre()` '#16161a' roughness 0.85; `make_whitewall()` (name 'wall') '#e8e2d0' roughness 0.7; `make_lamp()` colour '#fff1c0' roughness 0.1 emission '#ffe9a8' strength 6.0; `make_trim()` '#0e0e10' roughness 0.55; `make_tail()` '#c01818' roughness 0.2 emission '#ff2010' strength 1.5.",
   "Add `make_all(hexs, finish='solid', hex2=None)` returning a dict with keys `paint, glass, chrome, tyre, whitewall, lamp, trim, tail` (and `paint2` = `make_paint(hex2, finish, 'paint2')` only when hex2 is given). The old V03/V04/V05 code reads `mats['glass']`, `mats['chrome']`, `mats['tyre']`: keep those keys. Write `tests/cb_mat_test.py` (run under Blender): call `bpy.ops.wm.read_factory_settings(use_empty=True)` then `make_all('#C96F32','metallic','#202020')`; assert the exact Principled values above for every material (use abs diff < 1e-6), the paint Mix node A equals `carlib.hex_to_rgb('#C96F32')` and B equals A + 0.10 capped; with `finish='solid'` Metallic is 0.0 and B equals A; `MASK_RGB` has 9 distinct values; print `CB MAT OK`."],
  accept=[f"`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_mat_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB MAT OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB02", title="Studio world and colour management", needs=["V06"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_rig_test.py"],
  goal="A procedural studio sky for believable chrome and glass reflections, plus the exact colour-management settings.",
  read=[BL, API, CARLIB, PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/cbrig.py` (imports carlib; nothing runs on import). Add `setup_color_management(scene, exposure=-0.2)`: `view_transform = 'Standard'`, `look = 'None'`, `exposure = exposure`, `scene.render.dither_intensity = 0.0`, `scene.render.film_transparent = True`.",
   "Add `setup_studio_world(scene)`: a new world with nodes: Texture Coordinate `Object` -> Vector Math NORMALIZE -> Separate XYZ `Z` -> Map Range (From Min -1, From Max 1, To Min 0, To Max 1) -> Color Ramp -> Background -> World Output. Ramp stops (position: sRGB hex, convert with `carlib.hex_to_rgb`): 0.00 '#17121f', 0.44 '#2a2432', 0.50 '#e9dcc4', 0.58 '#b4c8e6', 0.80 '#5d80b8', 1.00 '#2b4a86' (the default ramp has 2 elements: set them to 0.00 and 1.00 and add the other four with `elements.new`). Background Strength 0.22. Assign it to `scene.world`. This is a dark floor, a bright warm horizon band and a blue sky, so chrome shows a believable horizon.",
   "Write `tests/cb_rig_test.py` (Blender): fresh empty scene; call both functions; assert the world has exactly one ColorRamp with 6 elements at positions [0,0.44,0.5,0.58,0.8,1.0] (tolerance 1e-4) and element 3 colour equals `carlib.hex_to_rgb('#e9dcc4')`; Background Strength is 0.22; `scene.view_settings.view_transform == 'Standard'`; exposure -0.2; `render.film_transparent` True; print `CB RIG OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_rig_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB RIG OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB03", title="Studio light rig and chrome probe test", needs=["CB01", "CB02"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_rig_test.py"],
  goal="The key / rim / fill / overhead-strip lights, tested by rendering a chrome ball and measuring it.",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbrig.py and tests/cb_rig_test.py (CB02) and tools/art/cbmat.py (CB01)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add `STUDIO_LIGHTS` = list of (name, hex, watts, size_x, size_y, location, target): ('top','#fff6e6',900,9.0,1.6,(0,-1.0,7.5),(0,0,1.2)), ('key','#ffe9cc',1400,4.5,3.0,(-6.5,-8.5,5.5),(0,0,1.5)), ('rim','#a9c6ff',800,7.0,1.2,(7.5,6.5,4.5),(0,0,1.5)), ('fill','#dfe8ff',220,7.0,1.5,(8.0,-7.5,1.2),(0,0,1.5)), ('stripL','#ffffff',400,1.2,5.0,(-10,-3,2.0),(0,0,1.0)), ('stripR','#ffffff',300,1.2,5.0,(10,-3,2.0),(0,0,1.0)). Add `setup_studio_lights(scene)`: for each, an AREA light (`shape='RECTANGLE'`, `size = size_x`, `size_y`), colour `carlib.hex_to_rgb(hex)`, energy = watts, at the location, aimed with `carlib.look_at(obj, target)`, named `name`. (Warm key from upper left front, cool rim from behind right, long overhead strip for the highlight along the shoulder line, tall strips for chrome.)",
   "Extend `tests/cb_rig_test.py` with a probe: after the CB02 checks, build a fresh scene: `setup_color_management`, `setup_studio_world`, `setup_studio_lights`, a UV sphere radius 1.0 at (0,0,1.0) with `cbmat.make_chrome()` (smooth shaded), an ORTHO camera ortho_scale 3.0 at (0,-20,1.0) aimed at (0,0,1.0), Cycles CPU 32 samples, denoise off, 128x128, PNG to `/tmp/cb_probe.png`. Load it with `bpy.data.images.load` (colorspace 'Non-Color'), take the pixels with alpha > 0.5, luma = 0.2126 r + 0.7152 g + 0.0722 b.",
   "Assert on the ball's opaque pixels: mean luma of the upper half >= 2.0 x mean luma of the lower half; max luma >= 0.95; at least 5 percent of pixels have luma > 0.9 (the softbox highlights); at least 10 percent have luma < 0.1 (dark floor reflection). Print the four numbers on one line `PROBE up=.. low=.. max=.. bright=.. dark=..` and `CB RIG OK` at the end. If a threshold fails because of the ball and not the code, report the printed numbers in your summary instead of changing the light table."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_rig_test.py 2>&1 | tail -2` shows the `PROBE` line and `CB RIG OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB04", title="Fixed-scale studio camera", needs=["CB02"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_cam_test.py"],
  goal="One orthographic camera with a FIXED pixels-per-metre, so every car is drawn at the same pixel density (a 3.5 m car is smaller than a 5.5 m car) and the ground point is always at the same pixel (the sprite anchor).",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbrig.py (CB02)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add constants `VIEWS = {'q':(25.0,10.0),'s':(0.0,6.0),'front':(90.0,4.0)}` (yaw degrees, pitch degrees; q = front three-quarter, nose toward the camera and to the right; s = pure side; front = head-on) and `CANVAS_W, CANVAS_H = 1600, 800`, `PX_PER_M = 224` (= 56 sprite pixels per metre x 4 supersampling), `ORIGIN_PX = (800, 680)` (where world point (0,0,0), the car's centre on the ground, lands, measured from the top-left).",
   "Add `setup_fixed_camera(scene, view)` (exit 2 with a message for an unknown view): ORTHO camera, `ortho_scale = CANVAS_W / PX_PER_M`, resolution 1600x800 at 100 percent. With yaw `ya`, pitch `p` (radians): target `T = (0, 0, 1.25 / cos(p))`; camera location `T + 20 * (sin(ya)*cos(p), -cos(ya)*cos(p), sin(p))`; aim with `carlib.look_at`; link to the scene and set `scene.camera`. Add `project(scene, point)` returning (px, py) pixel coordinates from the top-left using `bpy_extras.object_utils.world_to_camera_view` (py = (1 - y) * CANVAS_H).",
   "Write `tests/cb_cam_test.py` (Blender, no render needed): for view 's': `project((0,0,0))` is within 1.0 px of (800,680); `project((1,0,0))` x is 800+224 within 1.0; `project((0,0,1))` y is 680 - 224*cos(6 deg) within 1.0. View 'q': `project((1,0,0))` dx from the origin is 224*cos(25 deg) within 1.0 and `project((0,1,0))` has dx = -224*sin(25 deg) within 1.0 (nose side of the car is to the right). View 'front': `project((0,1,0))` dx is +224 within 1.0. A bad view name exits non-zero. Print `CB CAM OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_cam_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB CAM OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB05", title="Material mask render pass", needs=["CB01"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_mask_test.py"],
  goal="A second render of the same scene where every material is a flat known colour, so the pixel stage knows exactly which pixels are paint, chrome, glass, tyre and lamp.",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbmat.py (CB01: `MASK_RGB`, material names equal class names) and tools/art/cbrig.py", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add `render_mask(scene, out_png)`. For every MESH object except `shadow_catcher` and names starting `cut_`, replace each material slot's material by a new flat Emission material (nodes: Emission -> Material Output, Emission Strength 1.0) whose colour is the class colour of the ORIGINAL material name from `cbmat.MASK_RGB` (convert the 8-bit file value v to linear with the sRGB formula `v/255/12.92 if <= 0.04045 else ((v/255+0.055)/1.055)**2.4` so the saved PNG holds exactly v); an unknown name falls back to 'paint'. Remember the originals.",
   "Render settings for the mask only: Cycles samples 1, denoising off, `scene.cycles.filter_width = 0.01`, `view_transform 'Standard'`, exposure 0, `dither_intensity 0.0`, world Background strength 0, shadow catcher hidden from render. Write `out_png` (RGBA 8-bit, film transparent). Then RESTORE everything (materials, samples, denoising, filter width, exposure, world strength, catcher visibility) so the normal render can run afterwards.",
   "Write `tests/cb_mask_test.py` (Blender): scene with a paint cube (`cbmat.make_paint('#C96F32')`) and a chrome sphere side by side, camera via `setup_fixed_camera(scene,'s')` (CB04 may not be merged: if `setup_fixed_camera` is missing use an ORTHO camera at (0,-20,1) aimed at (0,0,1) with ortho_scale 7, 400x200), `render_mask(scene,'/tmp/cb_mask.png')`. Load with colorspace 'Non-Color'; among pixels with alpha >= 0.5 every rgb (x255, rounded) is exactly (255,0,0) or (0,255,0) and both occur at least 200 times; every alpha is 0 or 255; after the call the cube's material is still named 'paint'. Print `CB MASK OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_mask_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB MASK OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB06", title="Soft ground shadow render pass", needs=["CB03"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_shadow_test.py"],
  goal="A separate shadow-only render (a soft contact shadow as alpha), so the game can draw a real car-shaped shadow under any background without baking dark pixels into the car sprite.",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbrig.py (CB02-CB05)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add `render_shadow(scene, out_png)`. Create the catcher if the scene has none (plane size 40 at z 0, `is_shadow_catcher = True`, remember to remove it at the end). Set every MESH object except the catcher to `visible_camera = False` (they still cast shadows). Hide all LIGHT objects from render and set the world Background strength to 0. Add one SUN light: energy 4.0, `angle = radians(18)`, location (-1.2,-0.8,10) aimed at the origin with `carlib.look_at` (almost overhead, so the shadow sits under the car with a soft edge).",
   "Cycles 32 samples, denoising off, `film_transparent` True; the saved PNG alpha is the shadow density, RGB is irrelevant. Then restore everything (visibility, lights, world strength, sun and temporary catcher removed). Do not change the camera.",
   "Write `tests/cb_shadow_test.py` (Blender): `cbrig.setup_color_management`, `setup_studio_world`, `setup_studio_lights`, `setup_fixed_camera(scene,'s')`, a 1.0 x 1.0 x 0.5 m box on the ground made with `cbmat.make_paint('#C96F32')`; call `render_shadow(scene,'/tmp/cb_shadow.png')`. Load the PNG (Non-Color). Assert: max alpha >= 0.95; the count of pixels with alpha > 0.5 is between 2000 and 60000; their bounding box lies within x 800 +- 450 px; the mean luma of pixels with alpha > 0.5 is < 0.1; after the call the box has `visible_camera == True` and the light objects are not hidden. Print `CB SHADOW OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_shadow_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB SHADOW OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB07", title="Cut wheel arches and window openings in the body", needs=["V04", "CB01"],
  files=["tools/art/cbbody.py", "tests/cb_body_test.py"],
  goal="The V01-V04 body is a solid slab: the wheels sink into it and the greenhouse glass is buried inside it (checked by rendering t1_chimp). Cut real wheel arches and window openings so glass, tyres and chrome rims are visible. (If a shape card from another reviewer already does this, finish with `STATUS: BLOCKED` and say which card.)",
  read=[BL, API, CARLIB, "tools/art/blender_car2.py (how the body and parts are built), tests: docs/cars/t1_chimp.json (wheels, greenhouse)"],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/cbbody.py`: `cut_openings(dossier, body_obj)`. Build hidden cutter objects named `cut_w<i>` (Cylinder, 40 vertices, radius `wheel.r * 1.14 * U`, depth `(width_units + 4) * U`, axis along Y, centred at `(wheel.x*U, 0, wheel.r*U)`) for each dossier wheel (all of them, also a single centre wheel), and `cut_g<i>` for each greenhouse polygon (`carlib.extrude_polygon(poly, width_units*1.2, <any material>, name)`, then remove its modifiers). Set `hide_render` and `hide_viewport` True on every cutter.",
   "On `body_obj`: remove all its modifiers, then add one Boolean (operation DIFFERENCE, solver 'MANIFOLD', object = cutter) per cutter, in that order, then a Bevel (width `0.8*U`, segments 3). Do NOT add a Subdivision Surface: on boolean results it makes long streaks in the paint reflection. Keep smooth shading on. The glass objects (V03) stay at width `0.80 * width_units` (document that in a comment: the window opening is 1.2x, the glass sits recessed).",
   "Write `tests/cb_body_test.py` (Blender): load `docs/cars/t1_chimp.json`, build the body with `carlib.extrude_polygon` + `carlib.shape_body` if V02 exists (otherwise just extrude), call `cut_openings`. Evaluate with `bpy.context.evaluated_depsgraph_get()`: the evaluated body has more vertices than the original; for every wheel a ray from `(x*U, 0.25*width*U, 0.2*r*U)` along +Z has its first hit at z >= `1.10 * r * U` (the arch is open); a ray from the middle of the first greenhouse polygon's bounding box `(cx*U, 0.25*width*U, cy*U)` along +Y hits nothing (the window is open). Print `CB BODY OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_body_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB BODY OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB08", title="Whitewall tyres and two-tone paint", needs=["CB01", "CB07"],
  files=["tools/art/cbbody.py", "tests/cb_body_test.py"],
  goal="Two classic-car details done at material level: optional whitewall tyres and a second paint colour on the roof (two-tone cars).",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbbody.py (CB07), tools/art/cbmat.py (CB01), docs/CAR_DOSSIER_SPEC.md (`roof.colour_split`, `wheels`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbbody.py` add `build_whitewalls(dossier, mats)`: for each wheel whose dict has `whitewall` true (an OPTIONAL new field, default false; both sides, same placement as the V04 tyre, see `build_wheels` in carlib.py) add a cylinder of radius `0.90 * r * U` and depth `tyre depth + 0.05` units with `mats['whitewall']`, named `wall_<i>_<side>`; it sits between the tyre and the rim (rim radius is 0.62 r) so the tyre shows a 0.1 r black outer edge and the rim a cream band.",
   "Add `apply_two_tone(dossier, body_obj, mats)`: only when `dossier['roof']['colour_split']` is true and `mats` has `'paint2'`: append `mats['paint2']` to the body's materials and set `material_index = 1` on every body polygon whose centre z (object space) is above `belt * U + 0.4 * U`, where `belt` = the smallest y of all greenhouse points (the window line). Must run BEFORE the Boolean/Bevel modifiers are applied (they only evaluate at render time, so it is fine to run any time after the mesh exists).",
   "Extend `tests/cb_body_test.py` (Blender): a copy of t1_chimp.json with `roof.colour_split` true and `wheels[0].whitewall` true; `make_all('#C96F32','solid','#202020')`; assert that after `apply_two_tone` the body has 2 material slots, between 5 and 45 percent of polygons have `material_index == 1`, and all of those have centre z above the belt; `build_whitewalls` creates exactly 2 objects whose names start `wall_0_`; with `colour_split` false nothing changes. Print `CB BODY OK` (the same final line as CB07's checks, keep both sets of asserts)."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_body_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB BODY OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB09", title="blender_car2: studio rig, 3-view camera, materials", needs=["V06", "CB01", "CB02", "CB03", "CB04", "CB07", "CB08"],
  files=["tools/art/blender_car2.py", "tests/run_blender_car2g.sh"],
  goal="Switch the car builder to the studio look with new flags, keeping the old behaviour as the default so the older V01-V06 tests still pass.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/blender_car2.py (as extended by V02-V06), tools/art/cbrig.py, tools/art/cbmat.py, tools/art/cbbody.py", "tests/run_blender_car2f.sh (the style of the shell tests)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add flags to `blender_car2.py`: `--rig v1|studio` (default `v1` = everything exactly as before), `--view q|s|front` (default `s`; in rig `v1` the V06 values `side|threequarter` still work: accept both spellings, mapping threequarter to the old camera), `--finish solid|metallic` (default `solid`), `--color2 <#rrggbb>` (default none), `--samples <int>` (default 48). Bad values: clear message, exit 2.",
   "With `--rig studio`: build the materials with `cbmat.make_all(hex, finish, color2)` (replacing the dict V03-V05 use), build body, `cbbody.cut_openings`, glass, wheels, `cbbody.build_whitewalls`, lamps, `cbbody.apply_two_tone`; DO NOT create the shadow catcher in the colour render; call `cbrig.setup_color_management(scene)`, `setup_studio_world`, `setup_studio_lights`, `setup_fixed_camera(scene, view)`; render with Cycles CPU, `scene.cycles.seed = 0`, `use_animated_seed = False`, denoising on, the canvas fixed at 1600x800 (ignore `--w/--h`), file `--out`. Print `RENDERED <out>` and with `--report` the usual REPORT lines.",
   "Create `tests/run_blender_car2g.sh` (sh): renders `tests/fixtures/dossier_ok.json` with `--rig studio --view s --samples 8 --report --out /tmp/cb09_s.png` and `--view q` to `/tmp/cb09_q.png`; requires exit 0, `RENDERED`, `REPORT body`, that `file /tmp/cb09_s.png` says `1600 x 800`, that a bad `--view x` exits non-zero, and that the old default command (no `--rig`) still renders a 1024 x 512 PNG; prints `BLENDER CAR2G OK`. Run `sh tests/run_blender_car2f.sh` too (it must still print its OK line)."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_blender_car2g.sh` prints `BLENDER CAR2G OK`.", "`sh tests/run_blender_car2f.sh` still prints `BLENDER CAR2F OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB10", title="blender_car2: mask and shadow outputs", needs=["CB05", "CB06", "CB09"],
  files=["tools/art/blender_car2.py", "tests/run_blender_car2h.sh", "tests/cb_fractions.py"],
  goal="One command writes the colour render, the material mask and the shadow for a view, and a numeric check proves the glass, tyres and chrome are actually visible.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/blender_car2.py (CB09), tools/art/cbrig.py (`render_mask`, `render_shadow`), tools/art/cbmat.py (`MASK_RGB`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `--mask <png>` and `--shadow-out <png>` to `blender_car2.py` (only valid with `--rig studio`: otherwise exit 2 with a message). Order inside one run: colour render to `--out`, then `cbrig.render_mask(scene, mask)`, then `cbrig.render_shadow(scene, shadow)`; print `MASKED <path>` and `SHADOWED <path>`.",
   "Create `tests/cb_fractions.py` (Blender python; argument after `--`: a mask PNG path): load with 'Non-Color', classify the pixels with alpha >= 0.5 to the nearest colour of `cbmat.MASK_RGB`, print one line `FRACTIONS paint=0.644 chrome=.. glass=.. tyre=.. wall=.. lamp=.. trim=.. tail=.. paint2=.. opaque=<count>` (fractions of opaque pixels, 3 decimals).",
   "Create `tests/run_blender_car2h.sh` (sh): render `docs/cars/t1_chimp.json` with `--rig studio --view s --samples 8 --out /tmp/cb10.png --mask /tmp/cb10_m.png --shadow-out /tmp/cb10_sh.png`; require the three files and `MASKED`/`SHADOWED`; run `cb_fractions.py` on the mask and with python3 parse the FRACTIONS line: paint between 0.40 and 0.80, glass >= 0.08, tyre + wall >= 0.025, chrome >= 0.02, opaque pixel count between 60000 and 400000; print `BLENDER CAR2H OK`. (These bounds come from a hand test on t1_chimp with openings cut: paint 0.64, glass 0.19, tyre 0.035, chrome 0.058.)"],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_blender_car2h.sh` prints `BLENDER CAR2H OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB11", title="Pixel stage: palette ramps", needs=["A04"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_test.py"],
  goal="The hand-authored colour ramps for the pixel stage (pure numpy, no bpy): the paint ramp is generated from the car's exact sRGB hex so the colour is always right; chrome, glass, rubber and lamps use fixed ramps.",
  read=[BL, RAMP, "tools/art/pixelate.py (copy `srgb_to_linear`, `linear_to_srgb`, `LUMA`: do NOT import it, pixramp.py must not import bpy)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixramp.py` (numpy only). Copy the two sRGB/linear functions and `LUMA = [0.2126,0.7152,0.0722]`. Add `hex_rgb(s)` -> float sRGB array 0..1; `lin_hex(lin3)` -> '#rrggbb' (round to 8 bit after `linear_to_srgb`); the constants of the RAMP text above (`MULT, SW, HW, SHADOW_TINT='#2a1a5c', CREAM='#fff2cc', OUTLINE='#120a1e'`).",
   "`paint_ramp(hexs)` -> (7,3) LINEAR array. Base colour: if its largest sRGB channel < 0x20/255 use '#202028' instead (near-black paints still need visible shading). `b` = linear base; for i in 0..6: `c = clip(b * MULT[i], 0, 1)`; shadow tint that keeps luma: `c = c + (tint_lin * (c.luma / tint_lin.luma) - c) * SW[i]`; highlight: `c = c*(1-HW[i]) + top*HW[i]` with `top` = white if `b.luma >= 0.5` else the cream (linear); clip to 0..1. Step 4 therefore equals the base colour.",
   "`FIXED_HEX` dict: chrome ['#1b1830','#3c3f66','#7a86b0','#b7c6e2','#eaf2ff','#ffffff']; glass ['#0c141c','#1d2f3d','#4a6f86','#bfe0ee']; tyre ['#0d0d12','#1a1a22','#2b2b36']; wall ['#8f8a7c','#cfc8b4','#f2ecd8']; lamp ['#ffd870','#fffbe0']; trim ['#08080c','#1c1c26']; tail ['#7a0e10','#e02418']. `fixed_ramp(name)` -> (k,3) linear. Also `FADE=[0.0,0.12,0.30,0.50]`, `fade_hex(hexs, level)`: mix the sRGB colour toward '#b8aa96' by `FADE[level]`, return '#rrggbb' (level 0 returns the input unchanged; level outside 0..3 raises ValueError).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_test.py` (run under Blender): for hex in '#C96F32','#b02a2a','#3b4a2f','#1c2a6e','#e5e0cf': `lin_hex(paint_ramp(h)[4]) == h.lower()`; 7 distinct rows; luma non-decreasing; luma of row 0 < 0.04 and of row 6 > 0.45. '#101010': row 4 equals '#202028' (lift). '#ffffff': at least 5 distinct rows and non-decreasing luma. Sizes of the fixed ramps (6,4,3,3,2,2,2); `fade_hex('#C96F32', 3)` equals the formula; `fade_hex(h,0) == h`; level 4 raises. Print `PIXRAMP OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB12", title="Pixel stage: material class map", needs=["CB01", "CB11"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_class_test.py"],
  goal="Turn the full-size mask image into one material class per sprite pixel by majority vote (no coloured fringes at wheel arches).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11), tools/art/cbmat.py (`MASK_RGB`), tools/art/pixelate.py (`box_weights`: reuse the idea, do not import)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `pixramp.py` add `CLASS_NAMES = ['paint','chrome','glass','tyre','wall','lamp','trim','tail','paint2']` and `MASK_FILE = {...}` = the same 9 colours as `cbmat.MASK_RGB` written as float triples value/255 (copy the table; the test below asserts the two agree).",
   "Add `class_map(mask_rgba, out_w, out_h)`: `mask_rgba` is a (h,w,4) float array of RAW file values 0..1 (no colour conversion), top row first. For every source pixel with alpha >= 0.5 find the nearest class colour (squared distance); build a one-hot (h,w,9) array; area-average it down to (out_h,out_w,9) with the box weights as in pixelate.py (`box_weights` logic: each destination pixel covers `src/dst` source pixels, partial overlaps weighted); the class of a destination pixel is the argmax of that average; return an int array (out_h,out_w) and also a bool array `opaque` (out_h,out_w) = the area-averaged alpha >= 0.5.",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_class_test.py` (Blender, imports `cbmat` too to compare tables: `cbmat` imports carlib which imports bpy, fine): assert `MASK_FILE[k] == tuple(v/255 for v in cbmat.MASK_RGB[k])` for the 9 names. Synthetic 40x40 mask: left 20 columns class paint (255,0,0), right 20 columns glass (0,0,255), alpha 1; `class_map(...,10,10)` -> columns 0-4 are 0 and columns 5-9 are 2. A mask where one 4x4 source block is 10 paint pixels and 6 chrome pixels maps to paint; a block with alpha 0 everywhere has opaque False. A pure tail colour (0,0,0) with alpha 1 maps to class 7. Print `PIXRAMP CLASS OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_class_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP CLASS OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB13", title="Pixel stage: ramp quantiser with ordered dither", needs=["CB11", "CB12"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_quant_test.py"],
  goal="The core of the look: every material pixel is snapped to its material's ramp; paint uses the render only for the shading, so the colour is exact; a light 4x4 ordered dither softens the bands (the RSAMH look).",
  read=[BL, RAMP, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11, CB12), tools/art/pixelate.py (`BAYER4`, `nearest`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `bayer4()` (the 4x4 matrix of pixelate.py as floats, minus 0.5 so it is centred on 0). Add `quantise(rgb, opaque, cls, color, color2=None, dither=0.5, steps=(0,1,2,3,4,5,6), glass_dither=0.3)` -> `(out, idx)`: `rgb` (h,w,3) LINEAR area-averaged render colour, `opaque` bool, `cls` int class map, `color` the paint hex, `color2` hex for class 8 (paint2; if None paint2 pixels use `color`). `out` (h,w,3) linear, `idx` (h,w) int with the ramp step index (0..6) for paint and paint2 pixels and -1 elsewhere.",
   "Paint and paint2 (each with its own ramp from `paint_ramp` and its own median): `r = ln(max(luma, 1e-5) / median_luma_of_that_class)`; step boundaries `bnd[j] = (ln MULT[j] + ln MULT[j+1]) / 2`; `bw = mean(diff(ln MULT))`; `i = searchsorted(bnd, r + bayer4[y%4, x%4] * dither * bw)` clipped to 0..6; if `steps` omits some indices, snap `i` to the nearest allowed index (so the mini sprite can use fewer shades). Other classes use `fixed_ramp(name)`: `s = linear_to_srgb(luma)`; chrome edges [0.10,0.25,0.50,0.72,0.92] (6 steps, the top step is pure white); every other class: edges = `linspace(p2, p99, k+1)[1:-1]` of the class's own `s` values (p2/p99 = 2nd and 99th percentile); add `bayer4 * 0.08 * glass_dither` to `s` before `searchsorted` for glass and tyre only (dither 0 for chrome, lamp, trim, tail, wall). Classes with fewer than 4 pixels are skipped (left black).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_quant_test.py` (Blender): a 32x64 array whose paint luma ramps from 0.02 to 0.8 across columns (colour = '#C96F32' linear scaled), cls all 0, opaque all True. With `dither=0`: at most 7 distinct output colours; every output colour is a row of `paint_ramp('#C96F32')`; the pixel at the median luma has idx 4 and equals `paint_ramp(...)[4]`; idx is non-decreasing along each row. With `dither=0.5` the number of pixels whose idx differs from the dither-0 result is between 2 and 30 percent. With `steps=(1,3,4,5,6)` only those indices occur. A chrome class patch (cls 1) with luma 0.99 (sRGB 0.99 -> linear) maps to pure white; with luma 0.05 to the first chrome colour. Print `PIXRAMP QUANT OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_quant_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP QUANT OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB14", title="Pixel stage: selective outlines", needs=["CB13"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_outline_test.py"],
  goal="Heavy dark outlines like the 1989 game, but selective: a 1 px silhouette outline, inner lines between paint and glass/tyre, and NO outline across chrome or lamps so highlights stay crisp.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11-CB13), tools/art/pixelate.py (`add_outline` pads by 1 px: same convention)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `add_outlines(out, cls, opaque, color, color2=None)` -> `(rgb, alpha)` both padded by 1 pixel on every side (sizes h+2, w+2). Silhouette: every transparent pixel 4-neighbour to an opaque pixel becomes `OUTLINE` (linear) with alpha 1.",
   "Inner lines: an opaque pixel of class paint or paint2 that has a 4-neighbour of a DIFFERENT class (and that neighbour is opaque and not chrome, lamp or tail) is set to step 0 of its own ramp (`paint_ramp(color)[0]`, or `color2` for paint2); an opaque pixel of class glass or wall with such a neighbour of a different class (not chrome, lamp, tail) is set to the glass ramp step 0. Chrome, lamp, tail and trim pixels are never changed, and no inner line is drawn between two chrome pixels. Use the ORIGINAL classes for all decisions (do not let a changed pixel influence its neighbour).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_outline_test.py` (Blender): a 12x12 sprite, paint block 2..9 x 2..9 with a chrome 3x3 at rows 4..6, columns 4..6, plus a glass 2x4 strip in rows 3..4, columns 7..9. Assert: output is 14x14; the whole ring around the opaque bounding box is `OUTLINE` with alpha 1 and everything beyond is alpha 0; the paint pixels directly beside the chrome block are `paint_ramp(color)[0]` (inner line) but the chrome pixels are unchanged; pixels of the paint block that touch only paint are unchanged; the glass pixels touching paint are the first glass colour. Print `PIXRAMP OUTLINE OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_outline_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP OUTLINE OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB15", title="pixelate2: render + mask to a ramp-palette sprite", needs=["CB12", "CB13", "CB14"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate2.py", "tests/pixelate2_fixture.py", "tests/run_pixelate2.sh"],
  goal="The new command-line pixel stage: a colour render plus its mask in, a pixel-art sprite out, replacing the free k-means palette.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate.py (reuse its `load_rgba`, `save_rgba`, `downscale`, `add_outline` import: `import pixelate` from the same folder)", "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11-CB14), tools/art/check_sprite.py", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixelate2.py` (Blender python, `main(argv)` like pixelate.py). Arguments after `--`: `--in <png> --mask <png> --out <png> --width <int> --color <#rrggbb> [--color2 <#rrggbb>] [--dither 0.5] [--steps 0,1,2,3,4,5,6] [--scale <int>]`. Bad or missing arguments: a clear message, exit 2 (same style as pixelate.py `parse_args`; guard every `argv[i+1]`).",
   "Pipeline: `src = pixelate.load_rgba(in)`; `out_h = round(src_h * width / src_w)`; `rgb, alpha = pixelate.downscale(src, width, out_h)`; the mask is read RAW (write a small `load_raw_rgba(path)`: like load_rgba but without the srgb-to-linear step); `cls, opaque = pixramp.class_map(mask, width, out_h)`; `out, idx = pixramp.quantise(rgb, opaque, cls, color, color2, dither, steps)`; `rgb2, a2 = pixramp.add_outlines(out, cls, opaque, color, color2)`; `pixelate.save_rgba(out, dstack(rgb2, a2))`; with `--scale N` also write `<out>_xN.png` nearest-neighbour. Print `PIXELATE2 OK out=<path> size=<w>x<h> colors=<n>`.",
   "Create `tests/pixelate2_fixture.py` (Blender python): writes `/tmp/px2_in.png` (200x100 RGBA, a paint ellipse with a horizontal brightness gradient 0.3..0.9 in sRGB orange, a chrome disc, a glass rectangle, transparent elsewhere) and `/tmp/px2_mask.png` with the exact class colours of `cbmat.MASK_RGB` (raw values, alpha 0/255) for the same shapes, using `bpy.data.images.new` + `.pixels.foreach_set` + save.",
   "Create `tests/run_pixelate2.sh`: run the fixture, then pixelate2 with `--width 100 --color '#C96F32' --out /tmp/px2_out.png`; require exit 0 and `PIXELATE2 OK`; run `check_sprite.py /tmp/px2_out.png --max-colors 24` (must print `SPRITE OK`); a Blender python one-liner file `tests/pixelate2_check.py` (add it to your files) that loads the output and asserts: size 102x52, every silhouette pixel is '#120a1e', and at least 5 of the 7 colours of `paint_ramp('#C96F32')` (as 8-bit sRGB) occur; print `PIXELATE2 OK` at the end of the shell script as `PIXELATE2 TEST OK`. Also: a missing `--mask` exits non-zero."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate2.sh` prints `PIXELATE2 TEST OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB16", title="pixelate2: fixed scale, crop and anchor, sprite profiles", needs=["CB04", "CB15"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate2.py", "tests/run_pixelate2b.sh", "tests/pixelate2b_check.py"],
  goal="Sprites at one fixed pixel density, cropped tight, with the ground anchor point and size recorded: hero (HUD, TV, garage), mini (lists, HUD portrait) and face (head-on car 'portrait', like a character face in the 1989 HUD).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate2.py (CB15), tools/art/cbrig.py (CB04 constants CANVAS_W/H, PX_PER_M, ORIGIN_PX)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `pixelate2.py` add `PROFILES = {'hero': dict(ppm=56, dither=0.5, steps=(0,1,2,3,4,5,6)), 'mini': dict(ppm=26, dither=0.3, steps=(1,3,4,5,6)), 'face': dict(ppm=34, dither=0.3, steps=(1,3,4,5,6))}` and a flag `--profile hero|mini|face` that REPLACES `--width` (sprite width = `round(1600 * ppm / 224)`: hero 400, mini 186, face 243; the render is the full 1600x800 canvas, area-averaged down; hero is x2 on screen, mini x2 or x1, face x1 or x2). The old `--width` still works when no profile is given.",
   "After the outline step crop to the bounding box of the opaque pixels (outline included). Ground anchor: the original canvas point (800,680) maps to sprite pixel `(800*ppm/224, 680*ppm/224)` before the crop; after the crop the anchor is that point minus the crop origin (`ax`, `ay`, rounded to int, may be outside the sprite if the car is odd). For profile `face` pad the crop to a square (side = max(w,h)), centred, with transparent pixels, and set anchor null. Add `--meta-out <json>`: writes `{\"w\":..,\"h\":..,\"ax\":..,\"ay\":..,\"colors\":<distinct opaque rgb count>}`.",
   "Create `tests/pixelate2b_check.py` and `tests/run_pixelate2b.sh`: build (with a Blender fixture in the checker file) a synthetic full-canvas render+mask 1600x800: a paint block covering canvas x 560..1240 and y 440..680 (so the car's ground edge is exactly at the origin row), alpha 1. Run `--profile hero --color '#C96F32' --meta-out /tmp/px2b.json`: require size w=172 and h=104 (+-1): block width 680/4=170 plus 2 outline px, height 240/4=60 + 2... compute the exact expected numbers in the checker from the formula (`w = round(680*56/224) + 2`, tolerance 1) and require `ax` = `round(800*56/224) - crop_x0` (the checker recomputes crop_x0 from the block edge) within 1, `ay` equal to the sprite height minus 1 within 1 (the car stands on the anchor row). Profile `mini` gives width about 0.464 x the hero width (+-3) and at most 5 paint colours; `face` output is square. Print `PIXELATE2B TEST OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate2b.sh` prints `PIXELATE2B TEST OK`.", "`sh tests/run_pixelate2.sh` still prints `PIXELATE2 TEST OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB17", title="Pixel stage: shadow sprite and lamp positions", needs=["CB12"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_extra_test.py"],
  goal="Two pure helpers: turn the shadow render into a 4-level one-colour shadow sprite, and find the headlamp and tail lamp pixels so the game can draw additive glow (bloom) on them at night.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11, CB12)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `shadow_levels(alpha)`: `alpha` is a float (h,w) array 0..1 already area-averaged to the sprite size; returns uint8 (h,w) with values 0, 40, 90 or 150 using thresholds: alpha > 0.75 -> 150, > 0.35 -> 90, > 0.08 -> 40, else 0. The shadow sprite colour is always `OUTLINE` ('#120a1e'); write a function `save_shadow(path_writer, levels)` is NOT needed here (the writer is in CB20).",
   "Add `find_lamps(cls, opaque, min_px=2)` -> list of dicts `{'x': float, 'y': float, 'r': float, 'kind': 'head'|'tail'}`: 4-neighbour connected components (write a small flood fill, no scipy) of pixels with class 5 (lamp -> kind 'head') or class 7 (tail -> kind 'tail') that are opaque; skip components smaller than `min_px`; x,y = centroid (1 decimal, sprite pixel units, pixel centres at +0.5), r = `max(1.0, sqrt(area / pi))` rounded to 0.5; sorted by (kind, x).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_extra_test.py` (Blender): a 6x6 alpha array with values 0.9, 0.5, 0.2, 0.05, 0.0, 0.76, 0.75, 0.35, 0.08, 0.081 placed in the first row and others 0: output values exactly [150,90,40,0,0,150,90,90,0,40] (note 0.75 is NOT > 0.75, 0.35 not > 0.35, 0.08 not > 0.08); dtype uint8. A 10x10 class map with a 2x2 lamp block at rows 2..3, columns 7..8 and a single tail pixel at (8,1) and a 3-pixel tail line at row 8, columns 1..3: `find_lamps` returns one head with x 8.0, y 3.0 (centroid), r 1.0 and one tail (the 3-pixel line, x 2.5, y 8.5, r 1.0), the single tail pixel is skipped. Print `PIXRAMP EXTRA OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_extra_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP EXTRA OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB18", title="Condition variants 1: faded paint and dirt", needs=["CB13"],
  files=["tools/art/pixwear.py", "tests/pixwear_test.py"],
  goal="Wear states made at the pixel stage (condition 0-10 becomes 4 looks: 0 mint, 1 good, 2 tired, 3 wreck). This card: a deterministic pixel hash and the dirt layer (the fade is `pixramp.fade_hex`).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11 `fade_hex`, CB13 `quantise` returns `idx`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixwear.py` (numpy only). `hash2(x, y, seed)`: x,y int arrays; `h = (x*73856093) ^ (y*19349663) ^ (seed*83492791)` in int64, `h &= 0x7fffffff`; `h = ((h ^ (h >> 13)) * 1274126177) & 0x7fffffff`; `h = h ^ (h >> 16)`; return `(h & 0xffff) / 65536.0` (float array 0..1). `DIRT = ['#5b4632', '#3f3022']`; `DIRT_LIMIT = [0.0, 0.10, 0.24, 0.40]`.",
   "`paint_height(paint_mask)` -> float (h,w) `hf` = 1.0 at the top row that holds paint and 0.0 at the bottom row that holds paint (linear in between; pixels outside are irrelevant). `apply_dirt(out, idx, paint_mask, level, seed)` (modifies and returns `out`): for level 0 return unchanged; else a paint pixel becomes dirt when `hf < DIRT_LIMIT[level] + (hash2(x,y,seed) - 0.5) * 0.10`; the colour is `lin(DIRT[0])` when `idx >= 3` else `lin(DIRT[1])` (convert hex to linear with the function from pixramp, import it from `pixramp`).",
   "Write `tests/pixwear_test.py` (Blender): `hash2` returns floats in [0,1) and is identical on two calls; changing the seed changes at least 90 percent of 100 values. A 40x60 paint block with idx all 4: level 0 -> no pixel changes; level 3 -> every changed pixel has `hf < 0.46`, changed pixels are only the two DIRT colours, the fraction of changed paint pixels is between 0.25 and 0.55; the fractions for levels 1, 2, 3 strictly increase and level 1 is > 0. Print `PIXWEAR OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixwear_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXWEAR OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB19", title="Condition variants 2: rust, dents, pitting, cracks", needs=["CB18"],
  files=["tools/art/pixwear.py", "tests/pixwear_test.py"],
  goal="The rest of the wear layers, all seeded so the same car always looks the same, and all within the palette budget (rust adds 3 colours, pitting 1, dents and cracks 0).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixwear.py (CB18), tools/art/pixramp.py", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `RUST = ['#5c2a12', '#9a4a1e', '#c97a32']`, `PIT = '#6b5a48'`, `CRACK = '#bfe0ee'` (the glass highlight colour). `apply_rust(out, paint_mask, level, seed)`: `n = [0,0,5,14][level]` blobs; `rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)`; for each blob try up to 40 times to pick a random paint pixel with `hf < 0.5` (`hf` from `paint_height`); radius `rng.randint(2, 5)` for level 2 and `rng.randint(3, 6)` for level 3; the blob = paint pixels with `d2 <= r*r + int(hash2*3)`; colour RUST[1]; pixels with `d2 <= (r-1)**2` RUST[0]; blob pixels with `hash2 > 0.8` RUST[2].",
   "`apply_dents(out, idx, ramp, paint_mask, level, seed)`: only level 3: 3 dents; each picks a random paint pixel and for k in 0..3 darkens the pixel (y + k//2, x + k) to `ramp[max(idx - 2, 0)]` and lightens the one below it to `ramp[min(idx + 2, 6)]` (only where both pixels are paint). `apply_pitting(out, chrome_mask, level, seed)`: chrome pixels with `hash2 < [0,0,0.08,0.18][level]` become PIT. `apply_crack(out, glass_mask, level)`: only level 3: a diagonal line of 13 pixels (k from -6 to 6, `y = cy + k // 2`, `x = cx + k`) through the median glass pixel (cy, cx), set to CRACK where the pixel is glass. All functions modify and return `out` and do nothing at level 0.",
   "Extend `tests/pixwear_test.py`: a 60x100 synthetic sprite (paint block rows 10..49, columns 10..89 idx 4; chrome strip rows 52..54; glass rows 2..8, columns 20..60). Same seed twice gives identical arrays (`np.array_equal`), seeds 3 and 4 differ; level 3 colours are a subset of {paint ramp rows, RUST, PIT, CRACK, DIRT, chrome and glass ramp rows}; the number of distinct colours added by rust+pitting+dirt+crack at level 3 is at most 3 + 1 + 2 + 0 (the CRACK colour is already glass step 3); level 2 has 5 rust blobs: the count of RUST[0] pixels is > 0 and rust pixels all have `hf < 0.5`; level 1 changes nothing in these functions. Keep the earlier asserts. Print `PIXWEAR OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixwear_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXWEAR OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB20", title="pixelate2: wear, shadow sprite, lamp metadata", needs=["CB16", "CB17", "CB19"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate2.py", "tests/run_pixelate2c.sh", "tests/pixelate2c_check.py"],
  goal="Wire the wear layers, the shadow sprite and the lamp list into pixelate2, so one call makes a finished, aligned sprite, shadow and metadata.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate2.py (CB15, CB16), tools/art/pixramp.py, tools/art/pixwear.py", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add to `pixelate2.py`: `--wear 0..3` (default 0; the paint colour used is `pixramp.fade_hex(color, wear)`, also for color2), `--seed <int>` (default 7). Order after `quantise`: `apply_dirt`, `apply_rust`, `apply_dents` (pass the ramp), `apply_pitting`, `apply_crack`, THEN the outlines. Chrome mask = class 1, glass mask = class 2, paint mask = class 0 or 8.",
   "Add `--shadow-in <png>` and `--shadow-out <png>`: load the shadow render, take its alpha channel, area-average to the sprite size (same width/height as the car before the crop), `shadow_levels`, and write an RGBA PNG whose rgb is '#120a1e' where alpha > 0 and whose alpha is the level (values 0/40/90/150). The crop box must be the UNION of the car sprite's bounding box and the shadow's bounding box, applied to BOTH files, so they have the same size and line up exactly. `--meta-out` additionally gets `\"lamps\": find_lamps(...)` in cropped sprite coordinates (add the 1 px outline offset and subtract the crop origin) and `\"shadow\": \"<shadow png file name>\"` when a shadow was written. Writing alpha levels needs `image.pixels.foreach_set` directly (pixelate.save_rgba thresholds alpha): write a `save_rgba_levels` helper.",
   "Create `tests/pixelate2c_check.py` / `tests/run_pixelate2c.sh` (reuse the synthetic 1600x800 fixture idea of CB16, add a lamp block of class lamp and a shadow render whose alpha is a soft ellipse): (a) with the same `--seed` twice, `--wear 3` outputs are byte-identical (`cmp`); (b) wear 3 differs from wear 0 in at least 3 percent of paint pixels; (c) the distinct colour count at wear 3 is at most the wear-0 count + 6 and at most 38 (`check_sprite.py --max-colors 38` prints SPRITE OK); (d) the shadow file has the same size as the car file, its distinct alpha values are a subset of {0,40,90,150}, its rgb is '#120a1e' wherever alpha > 0; (e) the meta JSON has one `head` lamp whose x,y fall inside the sprite. Print `PIXELATE2C TEST OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate2c.sh` prints `PIXELATE2C TEST OK`.", "`sh tests/run_pixelate2b.sh` still prints `PIXELATE2B TEST OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB21", title="Pixel stage: all variants for one car", needs=["CB20", "CB10"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate_all.py", "tests/run_pixelate_all.sh"],
  goal="One Blender call that turns the raw renders of ONE car into every game sprite (views, sizes, wear states, shadows, metadata).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate2.py (CB15-CB20: call its `main(argv)` in a loop, or refactor a `process(...)` function out of it)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixelate_all.py` (Blender python): arguments `--id <car id> --raw build/art/raw --out assets/art/cars --color <hex> [--color2 <hex>]`. Raw inputs per view v in q, s, front: `<raw>/<id>_<v>.png`, `<raw>/<id>_<v>_mask.png`, `<raw>/<id>_<v>_sh.png`. `seed = zlib.crc32(id.encode()) & 0xffff`. Missing raw file: print the name and exit 2.",
   "Outputs in `--out` (wear levels 0..3, `Wn` means suffix `_wN`): from view q with profile hero: `<id>.png` (wear 0), `<id>_w1.png`, `<id>_w2.png`, `<id>_w3.png`, shadow `<id>_sh.png`; from view q with profile mini: `<id>_m.png`, `<id>_m_w1..3.png`, `<id>_m_sh.png`; from view s with profile hero: `<id>_s.png` and `<id>_s_w3.png`, `<id>_s_sh.png`; from view front with profile face: `<id>_f.png` (wear 0 only). 14 PNGs. Write `<out>/<id>.meta.json` = a dict `{\"hero\": {meta}, \"hero_w1\": {...}, ... , \"face\": {...}}` with the per-file meta of CB16/CB20 (keys: hero, hero_w1, hero_w2, hero_w3, mini, mini_w1..3, side, side_w3, face), each also holding `\"file\"`.",
   "Create `tests/run_pixelate_all.sh`: render `tests/fixtures/dossier_ok.json` for the three views with `blender_car2.py --rig studio --samples 8 --view <v> --out /tmp/pa/dossier_ok_<v>.png --mask /tmp/pa/dossier_ok_<v>_mask.png --shadow-out /tmp/pa/dossier_ok_<v>_sh.png`; run pixelate_all into `/tmp/pa_out`; require the 14 files, that every non-shadow PNG passes `check_sprite.py --max-colors 38`, that `dossier_ok.png` has at most 32 colours, that `dossier_ok_m.png` is narrower than `dossier_ok.png` and has at most 22 colours, that `dossier_ok_f.png` is square, and that `dossier_ok.meta.json` has all 11 keys. Print the elapsed seconds for the pixel stage on a line `PIXEL STAGE SECONDS <n>` and `PIXELATE ALL OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate_all.sh` prints `PIXELATE ALL OK` (it also prints the seconds; report them)."]),

 dict(id="CB22", title="render_all: studio pipeline, cache and manifest v2", needs=["V07", "CB21"],
  files=["tools/art/render_all.sh", "tools/art/build_manifest.py", "tests/run_render_all2.sh"],
  goal="`sh tools/art/render_all.sh [car id]` renders every dossier with the studio rig, pixelates all variants, skips cars that have not changed, and writes the manifest the game reads (the old keys `file`, `w`, `h` keep working for card S01).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/render_all.sh and tests/run_render_all.sh (V07, keep their style and the optional first argument)", "tools/art/pixelate_all.py (CB21)", "src/art/sprites.js (what the game reads from the manifest)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Rewrite the per-car body of `render_all.sh`: take `color` = first `typical_paint`, `color2` = second `typical_paint` only when `roof.colour_split` is true (python3 -c), `finish` = `metallic` when the dossier has `\"finish\": \"metallic\"` else `solid`. Cache: `stamp` = md5 (`md5 -q`) of the dossier plus `tools/art/blender_car2.py carlib.py cbmat.py cbrig.py cbbody.py`; if `build/art/raw/<id>.stamp` equals it and the 14 outputs exist, print `CACHED <id>` and skip (env `FORCE=1` disables the cache). Otherwise three Blender renders (views q, s, front, each with `--rig studio --finish .. --color .. [--color2 ..] --samples 48 --mask --shadow-out` to `build/art/raw/<id>_<v>*.png`), then `pixelate_all.py`, then write the stamp, print `OK <id> <seconds>s` or `FAIL <id>` and continue; exit 1 if any failed.",
   "Create `tools/art/build_manifest.py` (python3 standard library only): reads every `assets/art/cars/*.meta.json` and writes `assets/art/cars/manifest.json`: `{ id: {file, w, h, ax, ay, colors, lamps, shadow, wear: {\"1\": file, \"2\": file, \"3\": file}, mini: {file,w,h,ax,ay, wear: {...}, shadow}, side: {file,w,h,ax,ay, wear: {\"3\": file}, shadow}, face: {file,w,h}} }`; `file,w,h` at the top level are the hero wear-0 sprite so the old loader keeps working. Keys sorted, 1 space indent, ends with a newline. Deterministic: running it twice gives identical bytes.",
   "Create `tests/run_render_all2.sh`: copy `tests/fixtures/dossier_ok.json` to `docs/cars/dossier_ok.json`; run `FORCE=1 sh tools/art/render_all.sh dossier_ok` (requires `OK dossier_ok`), run it again without FORCE (requires `CACHED dossier_ok`); require `manifest.json` to have `dossier_ok` with keys `file w h ax ay wear mini side face shadow lamps`, the file `assets/art/cars/dossier_ok.png` to exist and `build_manifest.py` twice to give the same md5; then `rm -f` every dossier_ok file you created (docs/cars/dossier_ok.json, assets/art/cars/dossier_ok*, the manifest if it did not exist before, build/art/raw/dossier_ok*); print `RENDER ALL2 OK`. `git status --short` must show only your three files."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_render_all2.sh` prints `RENDER ALL2 OK` and `git status --short` lists only the three listed files."]),

 dict(id="CB23", title="Sprite checker v2: palette budget, outline and shadow rules", needs=["CB14"],
  files=["tools/art/check_sprite2.py", "tests/run_check_sprite2.sh"],
  goal="A checker the planner and the QA loop run on every sprite: colour budget per sprite, hard alpha, an unbroken dark outline, and the exact alpha levels of shadow sprites.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/check_sprite.py (same style: one result line, exit 2 on failure)", "tools/art/pixramp.py (`OUTLINE`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/check_sprite2.py` (Blender python). Usage `-- <png> [--max-colors 32] [--shadow]`. Car mode (default): load with 'Non-Color'; alpha must be 0 or 255 everywhere; the number of distinct opaque rgb values must be <= max colors (default 32); EVERY opaque pixel that has a 4-neighbour outside the image or transparent must be exactly '#120a1e' (255*round) EXCEPT pixels of the image border ring if the sprite touches the border (report them as `edge_touch`); count `paint_like` = distinct colours (the metric the planner uses to see banding). Print `SPRITE2 OK size=WxH colors=N outline=<count of silhouette pixels> edge_touch=<n>`; failure: `SPRITE2 FAIL <reason>` and exit 2.",
   "Shadow mode (`--shadow`): every pixel's alpha must be in {0,40,90,150}; every pixel with alpha > 0 must have rgb exactly (18,10,30); print `SPRITE2 OK shadow size=WxH levels=<n distinct alpha values>`.",
   "Create `tests/run_check_sprite2.sh`: use a Blender fixture file `tests/check_sprite2_fixture.py` (add it to your files) to write `/tmp/cs2_good.png` (10x10 opaque block with a '#120a1e' ring of 1 px, 6 inner colours), `/tmp/cs2_bad_outline.png` (same, one ring pixel '#ff0000'), `/tmp/cs2_many.png` (40 distinct inner colours), `/tmp/cs2_shadow_good.png` and `/tmp/cs2_shadow_bad.png` (an alpha of 60). Require: good passes (`SPRITE2 OK`, colors 7); bad_outline exits 2 and says `outline`; many exits 2 with `--max-colors 32` but passes with `--max-colors 40`; shadow good passes with `--shadow`, shadow bad exits 2. Print `CHECK SPRITE2 OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_check_sprite2.sh` prints `CHECK SPRITE2 OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB24", title="Contact sheet for visual QA", needs=["CB21"],
  files=["tools/art/contact_sheet.py", "tests/run_contact_sheet.sh"],
  goal="One picture of every car (hero sprite on a dark-purple floor with its shadow) so the planner and the Sonnet QA loop can look at all 31 cars at once and send fix notes.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate.py (`load_rgba`/`save_rgba` pattern), the file naming of CB21 (`<id>.png`, `<id>_sh.png`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/contact_sheet.py` (Blender python). Arguments `-- --dir assets/art/cars --out build/art/sheet.png [--scale 2] [--bg #4b5873] [--cols 4] [--variant w0|w1|w2|w3|m|s|f]` (default variant `w0` = `<id>.png`; `w1..w3` = `<id>_w1.png` etc.; `m` mini; `s` side; `f` face). Every car id is a file `<id>.png` whose id also has `<id>.meta.json` (list them sorted). Cell size = the biggest sprite's size times scale plus a 12 px margin; draw `<id>_sh.png` (alpha-blended) first, then the sprite, nearest-neighbour upscaled by `scale`; cells laid out in `cols` columns on the background colour (sRGB hex written as is); a missing variant file leaves the cell as plain background. No text drawing.",
   "Print `SHEET OK n=<cars drawn> size=<w>x<h> out=<path>`; no cars found: print a message and exit 2. Output via the same RGBA PNG writing as pixelate.py.",
   "Create `tests/run_contact_sheet.sh`: Blender fixture (inline in the shell script via a heredoc written to /tmp) creating 3 fake cars in `/tmp/cs_dir` (`a.png` 20x10, `b.png` 30x12, `c.png` 25x25, each with a `.meta.json` containing `{}` and a `_sh.png` of the same size with alpha 90); run with `--cols 2 --scale 2`; require `SHEET OK n=3` and that the PNG size is exactly `(2 * (cellw) )` wide and `(2 * cellh)` tall where cell = 30*2+12 by 25*2+12 plus no outer margin (check with `file`); a nonexistent variant for all gives `n=0` exit 2. Print `CONTACT SHEET OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_contact_sheet.sh` prints `CONTACT SHEET OK`."]),
]
